# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_alignof_alignas
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = _Alignof and _Alignas asserted by exact equality, with alignment observed only as residues
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No address is printed: alignment is observed through residues of an address modulo the
requested alignment and through 0/1 relations.  Each address is converted in two steps,
first to unsigned long, which is exactly pointer width on all four targets, and only then
widened to unsigned long long, so no conversion ever narrows.  Every object read is
assigned first, and no padding byte is observed.  No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
The alignments of double, long long, long and pointers differ per target - measured 4 on
i686 and 8 on the other three - so the exact expected value is selected by the
architecture macro the compiler predefines and every predicate is an equality against
that one number.  A permissive range is deliberately not used: it would accept an
alignment of 5, 6 or 7 and the wrong choice between 4 and 8, so a real defect would
still print 1.  The expectation is keyed to the architecture rather than to a
pointer-width macro, because the architecture is fixed by the target selection while a
width macro is a claim by the compiler under test, and checking that claim against
itself would be circular.  All four targets remain fully compared.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
alignof_stable=1 2 4 4
alignof_exact=1 1 1 1
sizeof_exact=1 1 1 1
alignas_struct=8 16
residues=0 0 0 0
readback=1 2 3
END
